The coefficient of determination (denoted as R2) is a statistical measure used in regression analysis to assess the goodness of fit of the 
regression model. It quantifies how well the independent variable(s) explain the variability in the dependent variable. In simpler terms, R2 
tells us the proportion of the variance in the dependent variable that can be explained by the regression model. 
Interpretation of R2: 
 Range of R2: The value of R2 lies between 0 and 1.  
o R2=0: This means that the model explains none of the variance in the dependent variable. The regression line does not fit 
the data at all, and the model's predictions are no better than just using the mean of the dependent variable. 
o R2=1: This means that the model explains all the variance in the dependent variable. The regression line perfectly fits the 
data, and the predicted values match the actual values exactly. 
o 0 < R2< 1: The value of R2 between 0 and 1 indicates that the model explains some, but not all, of the variance in the 
dependent variable. 
 A high R2 generally indicates a good fit of the model. 
 A low R2 suggests the model doesn’t explain much of the variance and might not be a good predictor. 
