Regression toward the mean 
Regression toward the mean is a statistical phenomenon that refers to the tendency for extreme values in a dataset to be closer to the 
average (mean) when measured again or observed in future instances. This concept is important because it highlights that outliers or 
extreme values are often followed by more typical or average values. 
It means that if you observe a particularly high or low value on a variable, it is likely that subsequent measurements will be closer 
to the mean of the population. This doesn't imply causality, but rather the natural tendency for values to "revert" to the average over 
time or with repeated measurements. 
Regression toward the mean is the statistical tendency for extreme values in a dataset to be followed by values that are closer to the 
average. It’s important to understand this concept in both interpreting data and making predictions, as it helps avoid overestimating 
the significance of extreme values and recognizing that they are often part of natural variability. 



